<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akaki Chkhenkeli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akaki Chkhenkeli was a Georgian Menshevik politician who headed the government of the short-lived Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic in April–May 1918 and led its delegation in peace talks with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. After the federation dissolved he served as foreign minister of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the Soviet invasion of 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akaki-chkhenkeli.jpg" alt="Akaki Chkhenkeli" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chkhenkeli_AI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Akaki Chkhenkeli was born on 19 May 1874 into a noble family in the town of Okumi, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a>. He studied at universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and became a lawyer and literature expert. He joined the Social Democratic movement in 1898, sided with the Menshevik faction in 1903, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a> and was briefly arrested afterwards. Elected to the Fourth State Duma, he advocated self-determination for the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. His younger brother was the linguist Kita Tschenkéli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 he served the Special Transcaucasian Committee as commissar for internal affairs and in June 1917 was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-central-executive-committee">All-Russian Central Executive Committee</a>. In March 1918 he led a delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> to negotiate with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batumi</a> and other land ceded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, who had not been consulted, refused to give it up, but the Ottoman delegation would not hear a party that was neither a signatory nor a recognised state, and by 10 April Chkhenkeli accepted the treaty as the basis for talks.</p><p>As prime minister of the Transcaucasian federation he formed a cabinet of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, five Azerbaijanis and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> and named five goals: a constitution, fixed borders, an end to the war, the suppression of anarchy and land reform. Under continued Ottoman pressure he urged Georgian leaders to choose independence, and the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> was formed on 26 May 1918. As its foreign minister he signed a treaty of protection with the German forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat the pro-German Chkhenkeli was replaced by the more pro-French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evgeni-gegechkori">Evgeni Gegechkori</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He led the Georgian delegation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> without result and was elected to the Constituent Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1919. Appointed minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 26 January 1921, he presented his credentials on 26 February, the day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>. He served as Georgia's ambassador in France until 1934 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in January 1959 after a short illness. He is buried in the Georgian cemetery of Leuville.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akaki Chkhenkeli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government was headed by the Georgian Menshevik <b>Akaki Chkhenkeli</b>, with Nikolay Chkheidze chairing the Sejm.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaucasian-democratic-federative-republic">Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Okumi, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.73,41.76&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Okumi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5560 646 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Akaki Chkhenkeli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6010 916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6010 916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.73,41.76&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
